  "step_3": "The decision reason evaluates which lottery is more right-skewed, which depends on the concentration of probability mass on lower (unfavorable) outcomes. Lottery B is more right-skewed (75% on 0) than A (50% on 0), so the reason would prefer B. However, the individual prefers A because it has a higher chance of a positive outcome (50% vs 25%), which reflects a focus on 'chance of winning' rather than skewness per se. While both considerations involve probability distributions, the individual disregards outcome magnitudes and focuses only on the likelihood of non-zero outcomes — a 'maximizing chance of winning' heuristic. The reason based on skewness would favor the opposite lottery (B) and involves a more nuanced assessment of outcome-probability alignment. The individual does not mention or appear to consider the imbalance between favorable and unfavorable outcomes in terms of skewness, nor do they weigh the relative extremity of outcomes. Therefore, the decision reason based on skewness was not used.",
